What to Drink During Your Period

What you drink during your period can be part of your overall self-care routine. Staying hydrated supports normal body functions and can help you feel refreshed throughout the day.

Water should be your main everyday drink. Keep a bottle near your desk, bed, or school bag so that you can drink regularly instead of waiting until you are very thirsty.

Coconut water can be another refreshing choice. It provides fluids and naturally occurring electrolytes. It can be enjoyable after exercise or during hot weather.

Water-rich fruits can contribute to hydration too. Watermelon, oranges, grapes, strawberries, and similar fruits can be refreshing snacks while providing vitamins and fibre.

Milk and suitable fortified plant-based alternatives can provide fluids along with nutrients such as protein. If you enjoy them, they can be included in breakfast, smoothies, or snacks.

Soups are another useful choice. Vegetable soup, lentil soup, and chicken soup can provide fluids while also adding nutrients to your diet. Adding vegetables and protein can make them more filling.

Some people enjoy warm caffeine-free beverages during their period. Warm drinks can become part of a relaxing evening routine. If you use herbal products, remember that natural products can still interact with medications or may not be suitable for everyone.

Tea and coffee can be enjoyed according to your personal tolerance. Some people prefer to reduce caffeine during their period, particularly if it affects their sleep or makes them feel uncomfortable.

Very sugary drinks can be limited if you are trying to build a balanced everyday diet. You do not have to completely avoid them, but water and nutritious beverages are generally useful regular choices.

Hydration needs vary. Weather, exercise, diet, and individual factors can influence how much fluid you need. There is no need to force excessive amounts of water.

Period care is also about feeling prepared. Keep sanitary pads available at home and carry a spare when travelling, working, studying, or shopping.

Choose a sanitary pad that fits comfortably and suits your flow. Regularly changing your pad is also an important part of maintaining menstrual hygiene.

